\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Fake news detection for low-resource languages such as Bangla is held back by
two things: the scarcity of large-scale, image-centric training data, and
classifiers that report only same-distribution accuracy without any external
evidence-verification stage. This paper presents \sysname, a two-stage
multimodal pipeline for detecting manipulated news imagery circulating on
Bangladeshi social media. The first stage fuses a BLIP-generated image
caption, encoded via multilingual SBERT and refined through Cross-Image
Semantic Fusion (CISF), with a CLIP visual embedding, and classifies the
combined feature vector with a gradient-boosted decision tree. A systematic
per-modality ablation on the BanFakeMM benchmark shows that CLIP visual
features dominate classification accuracy, reaching 95.58 percent alone, and
that no text-inclusive configuration exceeds this beyond fold-to-fold
variation; OCR, near chance in isolation at 56.80 percent, is removed. We then
evaluate under distribution shift with a protocol designed to be free of
selection effects: the decision threshold is chosen on a disjoint 150-image
validation partition and a sealed 200-image test partition is scored exactly
once. Zero-shot transfer to an unseen manipulation style costs 9.4 points of
accuracy and 23 points of fake-recall (95.40 to 86.0 percent, 95.75 to
73.0 percent). A validation-selected threshold recovers most of this without
retraining, reaching 92.0 percent accuracy and 95.0 percent fake-recall.
Supervised adaptation on 300 target-style images adds nothing measurable on
top ($+0.0$ points, 95 percent CI $[-3.5, +3.5]$); an earlier draft of this
work reported a gain here, which we retract as an artifact of selecting the
threshold on the evaluation set. The second stage is a retrieval-augmented,
dual-independent-LLM verification layer grounded in a Bangla-language
fact-check corpus, presented explicitly as a prototype.
\end{abstract}

