\caption{Per-modality ablation accuracy (repeated stratified 5-fold
cross-validation).}
\label{fig:ablation}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation} and Fig.~\ref{fig:ablation} report every feature
configuration under an identical protocol. Three results matter.

First, OCR text in isolation reaches 56.80 percent accuracy, close to the
50 percent chance baseline for this balanced binary task, with fake-recall of
only 32.55 percent. This is the empirical basis for removing OCR. We do not
generalize this beyond BanFakeMM: MultiBanFakeDetect \cite{multibanfake}
indicates that text-in-image remains a meaningful vector for Bangla
misinformation more broadly, and BanFakeMM's manipulations are photographic
rather than text-embedded.

Second, all text-only configurations saturate below 78 percent, while any
configuration containing CLIP exceeds 95 percent. Visual features, not caption
text or its derived properties, carry the discriminative signal for this task.

Third---and this is a negative result we report rather than obscure---no
text-inclusive configuration exceeds CLIP alone. CLIP-only reaches
95.58 $\pm$ 0.92 percent; adding CISF on top leaves it unchanged at
95.58 $\pm$ 1.01; the full caption+CISF+CLIP configuration reaches
95.40 $\pm$ 0.97, marginally \emph{below} CLIP alone. The largest gap among
CLIP-containing configurations is 0.18 points against a per-fold standard
deviation of roughly 0.9--1.0 points, so these configurations are
indistinguishable at this sample size.

% TODO(reviewer-4, BLOCKING): the fusion architecture is not justified by
% these numbers. Before submission do ONE of:
%   (a) demonstrate a measurable benefit -- run CLIP-only vs. fusion on the
%       sealed test partition of Table II, report a paired test and a
%       bootstrap CI on the difference, and add calibration (ECE/Brier) and
%       AUROC; if fusion wins on robustness or calibration, say so; OR
%   (b) simplify -- make CLIP + XGBoost the proposed model, retitle, and
%       demote CISF to a reported negative result.
% The paragraph below is honest but is NOT a substitute for either.
\emph{On the fusion design.} On same-distribution accuracy the caption pathway
earns its place only if it contributes robustness or calibration rather than
accuracy, which Table~\ref{tab:ablation} does not measure. Retaining a
multimodal architecture is not by itself a justification. We therefore report
the final configuration's parity with CLIP-only as an open question about the
fusion design rather than as a positive result, and treat CLIP-only as the
operative baseline any fusion must beat.

\subsection{Final Configuration Evaluation}

The selected final configuration---caption text fused via CISF, combined with
CLIP features, without OCR---reaches 95.40 $\pm$ 0.97 percent
same-distribution accuracy, with fake-class precision 95.10 percent, recall
95.75 percent and F1 95.41 percent. These are cross-validated means rather
than a single held-out confusion matrix, since the base-split protocol
(Section~\ref{sec:training}) uses repeated $k$-fold cross-validation rather
than one fixed train/test split; per-fold true and false-positive counts were
not separately retained, so a single confusion matrix for the base split is
not reported here.

\subsection{Performance Under Distribution Shift}
\label{sec:shift}

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Evaluation Under Distribution Shift. Thresholds Were Selected on a
Disjoint 150-Image Validation Partition; the Sealed 200-Image Test Partition
(100/100 Balanced) Was Scored Exactly Once. All Values in Percent.}
\label{tab:shift}
\centering
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Setting} & \textbf{$\tau$} & \textbf{Acc.} & \textbf{Prec.$_{f}$} &
\textbf{Rec.$_{f}$} & \textbf{Rec.$_{r}$} & \textbf{F1$_{f}$} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\emph{Zero-shot transfer (no target-style data in training)}} \\
Default threshold   & 0.50 & 86.0 & 98.6 & 73.0 & 99.0 & 83.9 \\
Validation-selected & 0.08 & \textbf{92.0} & 89.6 & 95.0 & 89.0 & 92.2 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\emph{Supervised domain adaptation ($+300$ AdversarialGen images)}} \\
Validation-selected & 0.21 & \textbf{92.0} & 90.4 & 94.0 & 90.0 & 92.2 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{p{0.95\columnwidth}}{\footnotesize Bootstrap 95\% confidence
intervals on accuracy (5{,}000 resamples): $[81.0, 90.5]$, $[88.0, 95.5]$ and
$[88.0, 95.5]$ respectively. Subscripts $f$ and $r$ denote the manipulated and
authentic classes.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_threshold_sweep.png}
\caption{Fake-class F1 against decision threshold on the 150-image validation
partition. $\tau^{*}$ is the argmax under the rule fixed in
Section~\ref{sec:training}; the sealed test partition plays no part in this
selection.}
\label{fig:sweep}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_ood_progression.png}
\caption{Accuracy, fake-recall and real-recall on the sealed test partition
across the three settings of Table~\ref{tab:shift}.}
\label{fig:progression}
\end{figure}

At the default threshold the classifier transfers poorly to the unseen
manipulation style: accuracy falls from 95.40 percent same-distribution to
86.0 percent, and fake-recall from 95.75 to 73.0 percent. The 23-point
fake-recall collapse, not the 9.4-point accuracy drop, is the operationally
meaningful number---27 of 100 manipulated images pass undetected while only
1 of 100 authentic images is misflagged. This asymmetry is consistent with a
decision boundary calibrated tightly around the training distribution's
specific manipulation signature.

Applying the validation-selected threshold ($\tau = 0.08$), without retraining
and without any further tuning, raises sealed-test accuracy to 92.0 percent
and fake-recall to 95.0 percent, at a cost of 10 points of real-recall. Almost
the entire shift penalty is therefore a calibration effect rather than a
failure of the underlying visual discrimination: the ranking of images by
fake-probability already separates the two classes well, and only the operating
point was wrong.

\emph{Supervised adaptation adds nothing measurable on top of this.}
Retraining with 300 additional AdversarialGen images and again selecting the
threshold on the validation partition ($\tau = 0.21$) also reaches
92.0 percent. The accuracy difference against the zero-shot model is $+0.0$
points with a bootstrap 95 percent confidence interval of $[-3.5, +3.5]$, and
McNemar's test on the 6 versus 6 discordant predictions gives $p = 1.00$. An
earlier draft of this work reported a 90.0 $\rightarrow$ 91.5 percent gain
from this diversification step; that gain was an artifact of selecting the
decision threshold on the evaluation set itself, and it does not survive a
disjoint validation partition. We report the null result rather than the
earlier figure.

\subsection{Confusion Matrix Analysis Under Shift}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_confusion_matrices.png}
\caption{Confusion matrices on the sealed test partition: zero-shot at the
default threshold (left) versus the domain-adapted model at its
validation-selected threshold (right).}
\label{fig:cm}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:cm} makes the trade-off directly visible. At the default
threshold the classifier misclassifies only 1 of 100 authentic images but
misses 27 of 100 manipulated ones. Under the validation-selected threshold the
domain-adapted model is substantially more balanced: 94 of 100 manipulated
images caught (6 missed) and 90 of 100 authentic images passed (10
misflagged). For a screening system whose false alarms cost a review step and
whose misses let manipulated imagery circulate, this is the operating point
the asymmetric cost argues for. The residual gap to the 95.40 percent
same-distribution ceiling is an honest bound on demonstrated behaviour under
shift, not evidence that the gap has closed.

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Baseline Comparison Under the Identical Cross-Validation Protocol.
All Values in Percent.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\centering
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Acc.} & \textbf{Prec.$_{f}$} &
\textbf{Rec.$_{f}$} & \textbf{F1$_{f}$} \\
\midrule
Linear SVM (TF-IDF)     & 76.30 $\pm$ 1.96 & 75.31 & 78.30 & 76.77 \\
XGBoost (TF-IDF + num.) & 78.95 $\pm$ 1.35 & 79.06 & 78.80 & 78.91 \\
\midrule
CLIP only               & 95.58 $\pm$ 0.92 & 95.12 & 96.10 & 95.59 \\
Proposed (final)        & 95.40 $\pm$ 0.97 & 95.10 & 95.75 & 95.41 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_baselines.png}
\caption{Multimodal configuration against text-only baselines under the
identical cross-validation protocol.}
\label{fig:baselines}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} and Fig.~\ref{fig:baselines} compare the multimodal
final configuration against two text-only baselines under the same protocol,
with all four metrics reported for every row. A linear SVM on TF-IDF features
reaches 76.30 $\pm$ 1.96 percent accuracy, and an XGBoost classifier on TF-IDF
plus auxiliary numeric features reaches 78.95 $\pm$ 1.35 percent---both
roughly 16--19 percentage points below any CLIP-containing configuration. This
confirms that visual features provide the primary discriminative signal for
this manipulation-detection task. We include the CLIP-only row deliberately:
it is the baseline the proposed configuration must beat, and at present it
does not.

\subsection{Key Observations}

\begin{itemize}
\item CLIP visual features alone match or exceed every text-inclusive
configuration on this dataset---the dataset's manipulations are photographic,
not text-embedded, so visual features dominate.

\item OCR contributes no measurable benefit on BanFakeMM specifically; this is
not generalized as a claim about Bangla misinformation broadly, since
MultiBanFakeDetect \cite{multibanfake} indicates text-in-image remains a
meaningful vector in this language more broadly.

\item The zero-shot distribution-shift gap (95.40 $\rightarrow$ 86.0 percent
accuracy, 95.75 $\rightarrow$ 73.0 percent fake-recall) is substantial even
for a well-performing same-distribution classifier.

\item Almost all of that gap is a \emph{calibration} effect: a threshold
chosen on disjoint validation data recovers accuracy to 92.0 percent and
fake-recall to 95.0 percent with no retraining at all.

\item Supervised adaptation on 300 target-style images adds nothing measurable
once the threshold is selected honestly ($+0.0$ points, 95 percent CI
$[-3.5, +3.5]$, McNemar $p = 1.00$). Where the threshold is tuned on the
evaluation set instead, the same comparison appears to show a 1.5-point gain.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Verification Layer Behaviour}

Unlike the classifier, the verification layer is evaluated qualitatively
through targeted case studies covering in-scope claims (relevant fact-check
evidence exists in the local corpus) and out-of-scope claims (none exists),
rather than through a live deployment trial or a quantitative benchmark. These
case studies were not sampled according to a documented procedure and do not
report inter-annotator agreement, so they are illustrative of the system's
operation under both conditions rather than a validated measurement of
verification accuracy or reliability.

A local demonstration application implementing this pipeline was used to
exercise both the direct-image-match and dual-LLM-verification paths during
development, but no live, sustained-monitoring deployment trial was conducted
for this paper; this is stated explicitly rather than implied.

% TODO(reviewer-5): add a worked-examples table here -- 2-3 real claims with
% the retrieved evidence, both models' verdicts, and the consensus outcome.
% The reviewer explicitly asked for "actual examples"; none are currently
% shown anywhere in the paper.

%==============================================================
\section{Discussion}
%==============================================================

\subsection{Visual-versus-Textual Modality Trade-off Analysis}

The ablation outcomes are explained by the nature of BanFakeMM's manipulations
rather than by any inherent weakness in text-based features generally. The
dataset's fake images are produced through pixel-level editing or full-image
AI generation, so the evidence of manipulation is encoded directly in visual
texture, lighting consistency, and generation artifacts---exactly the signal
CLIP's visual encoder is trained to capture. A caption or OCR-derived text
feature can only reflect manipulation indirectly, which is a much weaker and
noisier proxy; this explains both OCR's near-chance standalone performance and
CLIP's dominance once included. This also bounds the finding: on a corpus
where manipulation is carried by embedded text, the ranking could invert.

The absence of a measurable CISF contribution on top of CLIP is similarly
explainable rather than anomalous. CISF's neighbour-weighted fusion mechanism
is designed to sharpen a semantic embedding by borrowing signal from similar
items in the training corpus---a mechanism suited to text embeddings that
benefit from smoothing against semantically related neighbours. One plausible
explanation for CISF's limited effect on CLIP is that CLIP's visual embedding
already encodes a rich, high-dimensional description of the image directly
from pixels, leaving comparatively little redundant noise for neighbour-fusion
to average away---though this is inferred from the observed ablation pattern
rather than independently verified.

\subsection{Calibration Versus Representation Under Shift}

The distribution-shift results separate two failure modes that a single
accuracy figure conflates. A model can fail under shift because its learned
representation does not transfer, or because its representation transfers but
its operating point does not. Our results point firmly at the second. The
zero-shot model's ranking of shifted images by fake-probability is good enough
that a threshold of 0.08---chosen without ever seeing the test
partition---recovers 92.0 percent accuracy and 95.0 percent fake-recall.
What failed at the default 0.50 cut-off was calibration: the shifted fakes
receive systematically lower probabilities than the training-style fakes did,
so a boundary tuned for one manipulation style rejects the other.

This reframes what the 300 adaptation images were doing. Under a threshold
tuned on the evaluation set, they appeared to add 1.5 points; under a
validation-selected threshold they add nothing, because thresholding had
already captured the available headroom. Whether a larger or more diverse
adaptation set would help remains open---300 images is a small intervention
against a 1{,}600-image training set---but this experiment does not support the
claim that it does, and we do not make it.

We note the boundary of this claim: both splits come from the same benchmark
and differ in manipulation style (localized AI editing versus full-image AI
generation), not in image source or capture conditions. A shift that also
changes provenance, resolution or compression could plausibly damage the
representation itself rather than only the operating point.

\subsection{Addressing the Research Problems}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Training-data scarcity:} addressed through BanFakeMM's
adversarially constructed training set combined with an explicit, sealed
distribution-shift evaluation---rather than assuming same-distribution
accuracy generalizes to deployment.

\item \textbf{Claim-caption divergence:} addressed by supporting
user-supplied claim text as a first-priority input to the verification layer,
with the BLIP caption used only as a documented fallback.

\item \textbf{Evidence-source scarcity:} addressed by building a dedicated
Bangla-language evidence corpus, while explicitly acknowledging its
single-publisher composition as a scoping limitation.

\item \textbf{Missing verification stage:} addressed by adding a
retrieval-augmented, dual-independent-LLM verification layer with direct
image-to-image evidence matching, evaluated qualitatively and scoped as a
prototype rather than overstated.

\item \textbf{Same-distribution overstatement:} addressed by reporting the
zero-shot shifted result (86.0 percent accuracy, 73.0 percent fake-recall)
alongside the same-distribution result (95.40 percent), by selecting the
operating threshold on disjoint validation data, and by retracting a
previously claimed adaptation gain that did not survive that protocol.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Verification Layer Reliability Considerations}

Because the verification layer's central role is evidence-grounded claim
assessment, its reliability considerations differ from the classifier's. Two
design choices bound how much confidence should be placed in its output today.
First, the dual-LLM consensus rule is deliberately conservative: a verdict is
returned only when both models complete and agree, and any disagreement or
failure is routed to human review rather than silently resolved by one model's
output---this avoids overstating confidence, at the cost of leaving more cases
unresolved automatically. Second, the 0.90 image-similarity threshold used for
direct-match evidence was set from an informal empirical separation observed
during development, not a labelled precision/recall study, so it should be
treated as a practical operating point rather than a statistically validated
boundary. Both choices trade automation for caution; a production deployment
intending to reduce the human-review rate would need the quantitative
verification-layer evaluation identified in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}
before relaxing either safeguard.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Directions}
\label{sec:limitations}

Several limitations bound this work, in addition to the two disclosures
already stated in Sections~\ref{sec:data} and \ref{sec:verify}.

Ground-truth labels throughout this study derive from BanFakeMM's own
deterministic construction process, so the classifier's demonstrated
competence is specific to detecting this class of algorithmic image
manipulation, not to adjudicating whether a news item's overall claim is
true---a task the verification layer, not the classifier, is meant to address.
The distribution-shift evaluation, while sealed and leak-free with respect to
threshold selection, is drawn from a second split of the same underlying
benchmark; generalization to manipulation styles and image sources entirely
outside BanFakeMM, including organically collected real-world Bangladeshi
social media imagery, remains an open question.

The evidence-verification layer's evaluation in this paper is qualitative
rather than quantitative. A formal evaluation protocol---retrieval recall@$k$
against a labelled evidence set, verdict accuracy against ground-truth-labeled
claims---is left to future work; established RAG-evaluation frameworks such as
RAGProbe \cite{ragprobe} and the faithfulness, context-relevance and
answer-relevance metrics summarized by Roychowdhury et al. \cite{ragmetrics}
are a concrete starting point, alongside expanding the evidence corpus beyond
its current single fact-checking source.

Threshold calibration is a data-centric mitigation. Architecture-level
alternatives---CLIPood's fine-tuning-based approach to CLIP
out-of-distribution generalization \cite{clipood}, domain-specific
prompt-tuning approaches such as DPOD \cite{dpod}---were not evaluated here.
Threshold-independent metrics such as AUROC and FPR@95, and calibration
metrics such as expected calibration error and Brier score, were not computed,
although the results of Section~\ref{sec:shift} suggest calibration analysis
is exactly where the remaining headroom lies. Finally, this pipeline addresses
static image manipulation; extending it to video-based misinformation would
require a substantially different technical approach.

%==============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
%==============================================================

Practical Bangla misinformation detection demands more than a
same-distribution accuracy figure---it requires a distribution-shift check
that is free of selection effects, an honest account of which modalities
actually contribute, and, where a classification score alone is insufficient,
an evidence-grounded verification stage.

This research contributes: (1) a multimodal Bangla fake-news image classifier
combining BLIP, SBERT/CISF and CLIP features, with its final configuration
selected through per-modality ablation rather than default inclusion of every
signal; (2) an empirically justified architectural simplification---OCR
removed after ablation showed it performs near chance level; (3) a leak-free
distribution-shift protocol---disjoint adaptation, validation and sealed test
partitions, a selection rule fixed in advance, and a single scoring of the
sealed partition---which quantifies a real zero-shot gap (95.40 $\rightarrow$
86.0 percent accuracy, 95.75 $\rightarrow$ 73.0 percent fake-recall) and shows
that a validation-selected threshold recovers it to 92.0 percent without
retraining; (4) a retracted result, reported as such: supervised adaptation on
300 target-style images adds nothing measurable once the threshold is chosen
honestly; (5) a retrieval-augmented, dual-independent-LLM verification
prototype grounded in a Bangla-language evidence corpus; and (6) transparent
reporting of the concrete limitations of both components.

The gap between a high same-distribution accuracy figure and a system that
behaves reliably outside its training distribution is real and, on this task,
substantial---but our results locate most of it in calibration rather than in
the learned representation, which is both a cheaper problem to fix and an
easier one to get wrong when the operating point is tuned on the test set.

Two directions remain open. First, a paired significance and calibration
comparison is needed to decide whether the caption-fusion pathway is retained
or the classifier simplified to CLIP-only, since the two are currently
indistinguishable. Second, a labelled claim set with retrieval and verdict
metrics is needed to promote the verification stage from prototype to
evaluated component. Future work should also extend this evaluation to
organically collected Bangladeshi social media imagery and broaden the
evidence corpus beyond its current single publisher.

